# redhat — real-world examples

Setting up a RHEL server with Subscription Manager. Create a customer portal
account at access.redhat.com before or right after install; registration uses
that username and password.

## Register the system

```sh
subscription-manager register --username=user --password=password --auto-attach

# Confirm subscription status
subscription-manager status
```

## Other useful commands

```sh
# Unregister this system
subscription-manager unregister

# Remove all local subscription data
subscription-manager clean

# List repos (note: the subcommand is `repos`)
subscription-manager repos --list

# Enable / disable a repo
subscription-manager repos --enable=rhel-9-for-x86_64-baseos-rpms
subscription-manager repos --disable=some-repo
```

## Evaluation subscriptions

```sh
# In the customer portal, pick "Evaluate - Red Hat Enterprise Linux"
# from the downloads/subscriptions list, then attach:
subscription-manager attach --auto
```

## Homelab notes

```sh
# Free RHEL for up to 16 systems: register for the Red Hat Developer
# subscription with your portal account, then --auto-attach as above.

# Check which release you're on
cat /etc/redhat-release

# Patch a registered box
sudo dnf update -y && sudo systemctl reboot

# Dave's daily drivers are OpenSUSE Tumbleweed; the rough equivalents:
#   subscription-manager repos --list   ->  zypper repos
#   dnf update                          ->  zypper dup
```

## Flag reference

| Command | Meaning |
|---------|---------|
| `register --auto-attach` | Register and attach best-fit subscription |
| `status` | Overall compliance status |
| `unregister` | Release this system's registration |
| `clean` | Wipe local subscription data |
| `repos --list` | Show available/enabled repos |
